					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1777811&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606467085" alt="weizhong" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1777811)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;" >weizhong</a></span> <span class="odate time_1484714351 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Jan 2017 04:39</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2728757">
									

<p>I absolutely love, love, love the topic of alternate history, so I had high hopes for this article coming in, but there were just a bunch of things that didn't quite make sense to me (similarily to what TyGently and Cyantreuse both mentioned) that made it hard for me to stomach, such as:</p>
<ul>
<li>Somehow most of central/southern/eastern Europe experiences little change-which doesn't seem to make sense if Napoleon conquered their governments and replaced them. Even if they were conquered, things like Italy's unification and the formation of Poland were highly highly dependent on events in Austria and Germany that presumably would have never happened in this timeline.</li>
<li>France goes from &quot;collapsing&quot; to somehow being in control over most of Europe with no explanation, which is inconsistent.</li>
<li>For some reason France invades Great Britain and the Soviet Union for oil of all things, which makes little to no sense.</li>
</ul>
<p>Things like this really hurt the immersion of the article, and the randomness of 096 and a bunch of other skips being tossed in for what seems like name dropping reasons kind of kills it for me.</p>

							</div>

								
					
